data: {"id":"chatcmpl_ec928599-9524-4e5c-a48f-4c6ed01ccb7c","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792099396,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"Referee assessmentC1 is a special case of a known classical inequality, on the intended reading  $k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}$ . It should not be presented as an unresolved conjecture in that form. The decisive comparison is with the first lower and first upper Bonferroni bounds for a counting random variable, stated in Proposition 6.1, equation (6.2), of the primary source identified below. Institute of Statistical Mathematics+1No PDF was available in this conversation, and a Library title search did not locate a matching manuscript. This assessment therefore concerns the supplied C1 statement and source excerpts, together with the external sources actually inspected. It does not assess an unseen manuscript or a submitted proof.C1: well-posedness and mathematical meaningThe source model can be made explicit without introducing additional probabilistic assumptions. Its samples are\n$$s=(X,R),\\qquad\nX:A\\to Y,\\qquad\nR_a:A\\setminus\\{a\\}\\to Y.$$\nThe associated listing  $g_s:A\\times A\\to Y$  has diagonal entries  $g_s(a,a)=X(a)$  and off-diagonal entries  $g_s(a,b)=R_a(b)$ . The weights are column-indexed:\n$$w(s)=\n\\left(\\prod_{b\\in A}q(b,X(b))\\right)\n\\left(\\prod_{a\\in A}\\prod_{\\substack{b\\in A\\\\b\\ne a}}\nq(b,R_a(b))\\right).$$\nThe twisted diagonal is  $d_s(b)=f(X(b))$ , and the captured-row count is\n$$C(s)=\\#\\{a\\in A:\\ \\forall b\\in A,\\ g_s(a,b)=d_s(b)\\}.$$\nThese are the definitions in the inspected model sources, rather than a new model inferred from the word “independent.” GitHub+1The printed statement nevertheless needs an explicit domain and quantifier for  $k$ . For a standalone display, the natural formulation is “for every  $k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$ ,” with\n$$\\binom{c}{k}=0\\quad(k\u003ec),\\qquad \\binom{c}{0}=1,$$\nand the usual empty-sum and empty-product conventions. These conventions retain  $k=0$ ,  $k\u003e|A|$ , and empty underlying sets rather than silently excluding them. The JSON records this as an incomplete standalone formulation, not as a finding of mathematical falsity.No condition  $\\sum_y q(a,y)=1$  appears in C1, and none should be silently added. Consequently,  $T_k$  is a weighted mass, not necessarily a probability. Likewise,  $f$  is arbitrary: no injectivity or fixed-point-free assumption is stated.Known status and exact source comparisonFor a random variable  $N$  taking values in  $\\{0,\\ldots,n\\}$ , write\n$$M_j=\\mathbb E\\binom Nj.$$\nThe opened classical source gives the general tail truncations whose first lower and upper bounds are\n$$M_k-kM_{k+1}\\le \\Pr(N\\ge k)\\le M_k\n\\qquad(1\\le k\u003cn).$$\nThe threshold endpoints have their usual direct interpretations. Importantly, this is the at-least- $k$  formula; it should not be confused with the neighboring formula for the probability of exactly  $k$  occurrences. Institute of Statistical Mathematics+1The source matching is exact: the counting variable is  $N=C$ . With total mass  $W=\\sum_s w(s)\u003e0$ , the normalized readouts are  $M_j=B_j/W$  and  $\\Pr(N\\ge k)=T_k/W$ . Zero total mass must be retained as a separate case without division. This is a homogeneous change of normalization, not a model-specific estimate. The binomial-moment framework does not require independence of the counted events; the additional listing structure therefore does not create a new hypothesis regime for this bound. The inspected second primary paper also treats such bounds through finite-valued counting variables and binomial moments. Institute of Statistical Mathematics+2The repository comparison is narrower. The inspected escape_bonferroni_bounds declaration assumes nonnegative normalized cell weights and bounds the mass of no captured rows using single- and pair-capture sums. That supplies the normalized  $k=1$  comparison after taking the complementary event. It is not, by itself, an arbitrary- $k$ , unnormalized theorem. GitHubThe six diagnostic candidates supplied in the prompt do not establish the all- $k$  conclusion merely by being retrieval hits. The relevant excerpts provide model definitions, individual capture formulas, uniform counting results, and a uniform-weight bridge. No applicability correspondence was supplied for the causal-mediation or zeta-definition candidates. The positive classical-source match—not an absence of repository matches—is the basis for the status finding.Correctness, proof escape, information gain, and utilityCorrectness: not_checked, because no proof was submitted. This is separate from recognizing the intended assertion as a known special case.Conditional proof escape: bind_only relative to the inspected general tail theorem. Instantiation, expansion of the supplied definitions, normalization, and endpoint conventions already account for the unchanged conclusion. This does not claim that every conceivable new proof would lack interesting ideas; it means that C1 specifies no additional mathematical obligation beyond that baseline. There are no live proof witnesses and no proof-shape assessment.Utility: supported as an instance of a general theorem: it bounds a tail mass using two binomial moments. That is genuine inherited utility, but it is not evidence of a new research contribution, a faster algorithm, or an independently validated application. No artificial downstream consumer is needed to recognize the general estimate’s use.Information gain and settlement: no separate information-gain model or evidence was provided, and no named open-problem settlement was claimed. Both remain null.The appropriate revision is to supply the missing index conventions and self-contained definitions, and to label the result as a classical corollary. Any proposed stronger result or new application should be stated separately and compared on its own terms. This recommendation is feedback on formulation and positioning, not a publication decision.JSON{\n  \"recommendation\": \"revise\",\n  \"summary\": \"On the intended nonnegative-integer indexing, C1 is a special case of classical Bonferroni tail inequalities, not an unresolved conjecture. The standalone wording needs an explicit domain for k and complete sample and weight conventions. No PDF or proof was available for assessment.\",\n  \"strengths\": [\n    \"The intended assertion specifies a concrete two-sided tail bound from two adjacent binomial moments.\",\n    \"Nonnegative weights allow a finite-mass formulation without assuming normalized cell marginals.\",\n    \"The captured-row statistic has a direct correspondence with the counting-variable formulation of the opened classical baseline.\"\n  ],\n  \"concerns\": [\n    \"The printed statement does not explicitly quantify or type k; the known-status assessment uses the intended reading k in the nonnegative integers.\",\n    \"The assertion, unchanged, does not specify mathematical content beyond an existing general tail inequality.\",\n    \"The diagnostic retrieval receipt does not itself establish entailment, formal verification, or identity with the publicly inspected dev sources.\"\n  ],\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"C1\",\n      \"correctness\": \"not_checked\",\n      \"shape\": null,\n      \"witnesses\": [],\n      \"known\": [\n        \"Bonferroni-type inequalities; Chebyshev-type inequalities for the distributions on [0, n] [opened: https://www.ism.ac.jp/editsec/aism/pdf/043_2_0261.pdf]\",\n        \"Bivariate Binomial Moments and Bonferroni-type Inequalities [opened: arXiv:1511.06640]\",\n        \"FiniteProductCapture.lean [opened: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/dev/D5/S0/Asymptotics/WeightedProbability/FiniteProductCapture.lean]\",\n        \"EscapeCount.lean [opened: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/dev/D5/S0/Diagonal/EscapeCount.lean]\",\n        \"FiniteBonferroni.lean [opened: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/dev/D5/S0/Asymptotics/WeightedProbability/FiniteBonferroni.lean]\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"Correctness is not_checked because no proof was submitted. The intended assertion is a known special case. Its conditional statement-level escape is bind_only relative to the opened general tail theorem, not inferred from missing evidence. General-theorem utility is supported as inherited utility, while new research utili"},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl_ec928599-9524-4e5c-a48f-4c6ed01ccb7c","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792099396,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"ty and computational advantage are not established. No information-gain measurement or named settlement is asserted.\",\n      \"conjecture\": {\n        \"well_posed\": false,\n        \"well_posed_reason\": \"As a standalone printed assertion, C1 leaves k's domain and quantifier unstated and refers externally to the listing model. The supplied source excerpts resolve the model, and the intended statement is well-defined after declaring k to range over nonnegative integers, using binom(c,k)=0 for k\u003ec and binom(c,0)=1, and retaining empty-sum and empty-product conventions. This is a formulation defect, not a counterexample. No normalization, nonemptiness, injectivity, or fixed-point-free hypothesis should be silently added.\",\n        \"status\": \"special_case_of_known\",\n        \"status_reason\": \"For the intended integer indexing, the nontrivial range is the first lower and upper tail truncations in the classical source's Proposition 6.1, equation (6.2). The finite-mass correspondence uses N=C and homogeneous normalization when total mass is positive. Boundary indices and zero total mass require no new estimate.\",\n        \"named_works\": [\n          \"Bonferroni-type inequalities; Chebyshev-type inequalities for the distributions on [0, n] [opened: https://www.ism.ac.jp/editsec/aism/pdf/043_2_0261.pdf]\",\n          \"Bivariate Binomial Moments and Bonferroni-type Inequalities [opened: arXiv:1511.06640]\",\n          \"FiniteProductCapture.lean [opened: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/dev/D5/S0/Asymptotics/WeightedProbability/FiniteProductCapture.lean]\",\n          \"EscapeCount.lean [opened: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/dev/D5/S0/Diagonal/EscapeCount.lean]\",\n          \"FiniteBonferroni.lean [opened: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/dev/D5/S0/Asymptotics/WeightedProbability/FiniteBonferroni.lean]\"\n        ],\n        \"escape\": \"bind_only\",\n        \"escape_reason\": \"Relative to the inspected general counting-variable theorem, the unchanged conclusion requires instantiation and normalization rather than an additional model-specific construction or bound. This is a conditional classification of the statement's obligation, not an audit of an absent proof or a claim about every possible proof method.\",\n        \"suggestions\": [\n          \"State the sample space, column-indexed product weight, captured-row count, and universal quantifier k in the nonnegative integers explicitly; retain degenerate cases.\",\n          \"Present the bracket as a classical corollary with the exact tail-inequality source, rather than as an open conjecture.\",\n          \"Separate any proposed stronger theorem, new evaluation method, or independent application from C1 and provide its own precise comparison and evidence.\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"methodology\": {\n        \"baseline\": [\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"C1 assumes finite A and Y, an arbitrary function f:Y-\u003eY, and q:A×Y-\u003eR with q(a,y)\u003e=0 for every a,y. It defines T_k as the total sample weight over C(s)\u003e=k and B_k as the sample-weighted sum of binom(C(s),k). It does not explicitly declare k's domain.\",\n            \"location\": \"CONFIRMED STATEMENTS[0].statement, claim C1; also repeated in SOURCE CONTEXT. No manuscript PDF location was available.\",\n            \"source\": null\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"Samples are pairs (X,R), where X:A-\u003eY and R_a:{b in A:b!=a}-\u003eY. The listing has diagonal X and off-diagonal rows R. Its weight is product_b q(b,X(b)) times product_a product_{b!=a} q(b,R_a(b)); Captured means equality of a listing row with its twisted diagonal.\",\n            \"location\": \"Declarations OffRow, Sample, listing, sampleWeight and Captured\",\n            \"source\": \"FiniteProductCapture.lean [opened: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/dev/D5/S0/Asymptotics/WeightedProbability/FiniteProductCapture.lean]\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"For arbitrary f:Y-\u003eY and g:A-\u003eA-\u003eY, diagonal(f,g)(b)=f(g(b,b)).\",\n            \"location\": \"Declaration diagonal\",\n            \"source\": \"EscapeCount.lean [opened: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/dev/D5/S0/Diagonal/EscapeCount.lean]\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"For a counting variable N supported on {0,...,n}, let M_j=E[binom(N,j)]. The first lower and upper tail truncations give M_k-k M_{k+1}\u003c=P(N\u003e=k)\u003c=M_k for 1\u003c=k\u003cn, without independence assumptions.\",\n            \"location\": \"Section 2, Lemma 2.2, page 263; Section 6, Proposition 6.1, equation (6.2), pages 278-279\",\n            \"source\": \"Bonferroni-type inequalities; Chebyshev-type inequalities for the distributions on [0, n] [opened: https://www.ism.ac.jp/editsec/aism/pdf/043_2_0261.pdf]\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"Comparison-only repository coverage: for finite A,Y, an ordering of A, q\u003e=0 and sum_y q(b,y)=1 for every b, escapeProbability lies between 1 minus the sum of single-capture probabilities and that expression plus the unordered pair-capture sum.\",\n            \"location\": \"Declarations pairProbabilitySum and escape_bonferroni_bounds; normalized k=1 coverage, not an all-k prerequisite\",\n            \"source\": \"FiniteBonferroni.lean [opened: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/dev/D5/S0/Asymptotics/WeightedProbability/FiniteBonferroni.lean]\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"searched\": [\n          \"Conversation Files semantic query: bonferroni-bracket finite independent listing model T_k B_k C(s). The tool reported that no files were present in the conversation.\",\n          \"Library title query: \\\"bonferroni-bracket\\\". No matching result was returned; this is not proof that no such file exists elsewhere.\",\n          \"General web search queries: Bonferroni inequalities at least r events S_r r S_{r+1} binomial moments; generalized Bonferroni inequalities probability at least k events binomial moments two term.\",\n          \"Primary-source inspection: the classical baseline's definitions, Lemma 2.2, and Proposition 6.1, equation (6.2). Relevant PDF page images were inspected to resolve mathematical notation.\",\n          \"Corroborative inspection: Bivariate Binomial Moments and Bonferroni-type Inequalities [opened: arXiv:1511.06640], version 2, introduction and Section 2.2, Theorems 2-3. This was not needed as an additional premise beyond the direct univariate baseline.\",\n          \"Public raw GitHub inspection: the three model/comparison modules listed in the baseline, plus a limited repository import-root inspection. These reads concern dev, not an authenticated reproduction of the receipt's frozen snapshot.\",\n          \"The six supplied diagnostic excerpts were compared for relevance. No MathSciNet or zbMATH search, exhaustive repository search, or universal literature search was performed.\",\n          \"An attempted Project Euclid article endpoint returned no usable article text and was excluded from the substantive baseline.\"\n        ],\n        \"literature\": \"supported\",\n        \"binding_analysis\": \"Expand the supplied sample, listing and capture definitions, with C(s) equal to the number of rows satisfying the capture predicate. Put W=sum_s w(s). For W\u003e0, the direct correspondence is N=C, M_j=B_j/W and P(N\u003e=k)=T_k/W. The existing tail theorem then has exactly C1's coefficient and directions after normalization. Nonnegative cell weights supply nonnegative sample weights; capture-event independence is not required. Zero total mass, k=0, k=|A| and k\u003e|A| are retained through the finite-sum and binomial conventions. No positive-total or normalized-marginal assumption is added to C1. The repository's normalized k=1 comparison alone would not supply the general threshold statement.\",\n        \"delta\": \"The conditional obligation is precisely the stated bracket for every finite A,Y, every nonnegative q, every f:Y-\u003eY and every intended natural index k, including degenerate cases. Relative to the opened general tail theorem, no additional mathematical bound or construction remains. The outstanding manuscript tasks are explicit indexing, complete definitions, corr"},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl_ec928599-9524-4e5c-a48f-4c6ed01ccb7c","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792099396,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"ect normalization language and attribution. C1 contains no separately specified stronger claim.\",\n        \"escape\": \"bind_only\",\n        \"witnesses\": [],\n        \"utility\": {\n          \"kind\": \"general_theorem\",\n          \"status\": \"supported\",\n          \"rationale\": \"The estimate provides general tail-mass control using two binomial moments. That is legitimate inherited theorem utility, independent of novelty. The submission supplies no evidence of a computational speedup or a new application.\",\n          \"problem\": \"Bound the weighted mass of samples with at least k captured rows using B_k and B_{k+1}.\",\n          \"progress\": \"The requested bracket is covered by an existing general counting-variable inequality after the model correspondence and normalization are made explicit.\",\n          \"prerequisites\": \"A finite sample set; nonnegative sample weights; an integer-valued count C between 0 and |A|; the stated definitions of T_k and B_k; standard binomial conventions.\",\n          \"discharge\": \"Finiteness, the product-weight definition and the captured-row count are present in the supplied and inspected definitions. Source comparison identifies the existing bound. The manuscript must still explicitly declare the intended index domain. No submitted proof or computational artifact was checked.\"\n        },\n        \"settlement\": null,\n        \"information_gain\": null,\n        \"limits\": [\n          \"The escape finding concerns the conditional statement-level obligation relative to the opened baseline, not the structure of an absent proof.\",\n          \"not_checkable: the missing PDF, its notation conventions, page locations and any material not included in the supplied text.\",\n          \"The source excerpts and public dev files were read as mathematical source text; no Lean compilation, kernel verification or Trureturing registration was performed.\",\n          \"The exact frozen publication identity and build assertions in the diagnostic receipt were not independently authenticated.\",\n          \"Supported literature overlap is limited to this precise bracket; historical priority and exhaustive coverage of possible stronger variants were not assessed.\",\n          \"Utility is inherited from a known general theorem, not evidence of a new contribution. No information-gain measurement, escape percentage or AURIC/FIB measurement is reported.\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"limits\": [\n    \"Only one confirmed assertion, C1, was evaluated.\",\n    \"No PDF was available; the review cannot certify that the supplied statement reproduces a manuscript faithfully.\",\n    \"The special_case_of_known status applies to the intended nonnegative-integer indexing, not to an unspecified extension of binomial coefficients to other domains.\",\n    \"No proof or refutation was attempted, and proof correctness remains not_checked.\",\n    \"All opened-source identifications are model-reported, not venue-verified references.\",\n    \"The positive classical-source comparison supports the overlap finding; retrieval failures and omitted candidates are not evidence of novelty.\",\n    \"No named open-problem settlement was claimed, and no separate information-gain evidence was supplied.\",\n    \"The recommendation is editorial and mathematical feedback, not a publication decision.\"\n  ],\n  \"text\": \"Revise the formulation and present C1 as a classical corollary. Its existing general-theorem utility is distinct from novelty, while any stronger result or new application requires a separate statement and assessment.\",\n  \"knowledge_queries\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"C1\",\n      \"definitions\": [\n        \"Sample s=(X,R), with X:A-\u003eY and R_a:A\\\\{a}-\u003eY; g_s(a,a)=X(a), g_s(a,b)=R_a(b) for b!=a.\",\n        \"w(s)=product_b q(b,X(b)) times product_a product_{b!=a} q(b,R_a(b)); weights are column-indexed.\",\n        \"C(s)=#{a in A: for every b in A, g_s(a,b)=f(X(b))}.\",\n        \"T_k=sum_{s:C(s)\u003e=k}w(s); B_k=sum_s binom(C(s),k)w(s).\"\n      ],\n      \"hypotheses\": [\n        \"A and Y are finite.\",\n        \"q:A×Y-\u003eR and q(a,y)\u003e=0 for all a,y.\",\n        \"f:Y-\u003eY is arbitrary.\"\n      ],\n      \"quantifiers\": \"For every finite A,Y, every nonnegative q and every f:Y-\u003eY. C1 does not explicitly type k; retrieve the intended k in the nonnegative integers, retaining k=0, k\u003e|A| and empty sets.\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"B_k-k B_{k+1}\u003c=T_k\u003c=B_k. Under intended counting conventions binom(c,0)=1 and binom(c,k)=0 for k\u003ec. No normalization or capture-event independence is assumed.\",\n      \"search_terms\": [\n        \"Bonferroni tail inequalities\",\n        \"binomial moments\",\n        \"factorial moment bounds\",\n        \"at least k events\",\n        \"generalized inclusion-exclusion\",\n        \"finite nonnegative measure\",\n        \"arbitrary event dependence\",\n        \"first lower and upper Bonferroni truncations\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Retrieve the arbitrary-threshold bound with coefficient k, not the exactly-k coefficient, including finite-mass normalization and boundary conventions. Supplied diagnostic hits emphasize model definitions and normalized capture or escape formulas. This is one proposed query for the subsequent source audit, not an already-executed diagnostic query.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"},"finish_reason":null}]}

